% ---------------------------------------------------------- challenge --------
\section*{Project design challenge}
Develop a parametric, mission-profile-driven configurator in CATIA V5, controlled from a Visual Studio (VB.NET) GUI, that generates the internal \textbf{semi-monocoque fuselage} structure and the \textbf{landing-gear placement} of the UAV that Team Findus is building for the SAE AeroDesign Brasil 2026 competition. The wing and empennage of the same aircraft are being modelled in parallel by Findus Team 1 (Group 21).

% ----------------------------------------------------------- motivation ------
\section*{Motivation}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The fuselage internal layout (battery, motor, ESC, flight controller, payload bay) is the part of the UAV that gets re-arranged the most as the mission profile changes during the conceptual phase. Driving the layout from a mission-profile form removes most of the manual repositioning work.
    \item Semi-monocoque structures consist of repetitive bulkheads, frames, stringers and longerons -- a textbook case for PowerCopy reuse.
    \item Landing-gear position depends on the centre of gravity, which itself depends on the placement of the heavy payload items. Computing the CG inside the GUI and feeding it back to the gear position closes the loop and removes a hand-iteration that otherwise costs a full afternoon.
    \item A clean GUI lets the rest of Team Findus (aerodynamics, propulsion, manufacturing) try variants without opening CATIA, and a SQLite library of standard mission profiles makes the tool re-usable for next year's competition.
    \item PowerCopies + clean code + database integration map directly to the Grade-5 examination criteria.
\end{itemize}

% ----------------------------------------------------------- description ----
\section*{Project description}
The user enters a \emph{mission profile} into the Visual Studio form, payload mass and dimensions, target endurance, take-off run, motor / battery class and the configurator does the rest. The VB.NET layer reads the inputs, computes a fuselage length, a bulkhead spacing and the longitudinal position of every internal item from a small set of sizing rules, runs a CG calculation over the placed masses, and drives the CATIA V5 model through the interop API. Reference geometry lives in a single \texttt{References.CATPart} skeleton; PowerCopies for the bulkhead, frame, stringer, longeron and lightening cut-out are stored separately and instantiated programmatically along the skeleton.

The configurator is built around five reusable PowerCopies: a \textbf{Bulkhead} (parametric on fuselage cross-section, instantiated at every payload boundary), a \textbf{Frame} (lighter intermediate ring between bulkheads, instantiated along the fuselage), a \textbf{Stringer} and \textbf{Longeron} pair (longitudinal stiffeners, instantiated around the perimeter), and a \textbf{Lightening cut-out} (instantiated on every bulkhead and frame for weight efficiency). The landing-gear strut + wheel pair is instantiated upon the strut PowerCopy at the X-station produced by the CG solver this is the instantiation-upon-instantiation case required for Grade 4. A SQLite database stores standard mission profiles (\emph{light cargo, endurance, max-payload}) so the user can save, reload and compare variants from the GUI.

